Origin of the fixtures in this directory
========================================

The .rar files here are NOT third-party test data. They were written by
gen/main.go in this directory, which hand-assembles RAR 1.5 ("rar4") and RAR 5
("rar5") archives containing stored (uncompressed) files.

The original plan was to copy small archives out of github.com/nwaples/rardecode's
own testdata, which is BSD-2-Clause licensed. That is not possible: the module
zip that `go get` fetches contains no testdata directory, so there is nothing in
the module cache to copy. Verify with:

    ls "$(go env GOMODCACHE)/github.com/nwaples/rardecode/v2@"*/

Building the fixtures instead of copying them is only tractable because rar's
*stored* method needs no encoder — the header layout is documented and the
payload is the file's bytes verbatim. The fixtures are therefore fully covered
by this repository's own licence, and every one of them is decoded by
github.com/nwaples/rardecode/v2 in extract's tests, which is the check that
they are honest rar files rather than something only our own code accepts.

Regenerate after editing gen/main.go:

    go run ./internal/usenet/extract/testdata/gen

MANIFEST.json records, for each fixture set, the volume filenames and the size
and SHA-256 of every file the set is supposed to yield. The tests read it
rather than hard-coding expectations next to the generator.

Fixture sets
------------

  single            single.rar — two files and a nested directory
  multi-new         multi.part01.rar .. multi.part03.rar — modern volume
                    naming, one file wholly inside volume one and one file
                    split across all three
  multi-mixed       mixed.part1.rar, mixed.part02.rar, mixed.part03.rar,
                    mixed.part04.rar — the only rar5 set here, because rar5 is
                    the only format that records a volume's own number. Both
                    real-world naming defects at once: part1 is a digit
                    narrower than the rest, and the files called part03 and
                    part04 hold each other's volumes. Reading the set by name
                    produces rardecode's "bad volume number" partway through
                    the payload
  multi-old         old.rar, old.r00, old.r01 — legacy volume naming, one file
                    split across all three
  file-encrypted    fileenc.rar — a file header flagged encrypted
  header-encrypted  headerenc.rar — an archive header flagged encrypted, which
                    rardecode rejects at open time rather than per entry
  corrupt           corrupt.rar — a stored file whose recorded CRC32 does not
                    match its bytes

Zip fixtures are not committed: archive/zip can write them, so the tests build
those in a temp directory instead.
